  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1248 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\n**** Mid-Week Rainfall ****\n\nAfter a period of quiet weather with high temperatures in the \n60s through Tuesday, a storm system will move into the area on \nWednesday. All 12z models are in good agreement concerning the \nstrength/timing of the system, with all solutions showing two \nshort-wave troughs phasing into a significant upper trough east \nof the Mississippi River. All models also suggest the \nassociated precip will not start until after the phasing gets \ngoing, which means part of the area will remain mostly dry. The\ncorresponding surface low will track eastward and deepen over \ncentral Illinois on Wednesday, leading to the next chance for \nprecipitation. Strong upper dynamics will be in place and the \nGFS is showing PW values increasing to around 1 inch ahead of \nthe low. Therefore, confidence is growing that showers will \nbecome widespread during the afternoon and evening, especially \nin the northeastern and eastern parts of the CWA. This is \nreflected with NBM PoPs of 20-40% in the western half of the CWA\nand 40-60% in the eastern half of the CWA. In addition, a \nnarrow band of instability characterized by MUCAPEs of around \n500 J/kg will support the potential for a few rumbles of thunder.\nOnce the low passes to the east, the bulk of the showers will \npush into the Ohio River Valley Wednesday night. After that, \nattention will turn to the increasingly cyclonic flow aloft and \na band of lake-enhanced showers from Lake Michigan on Thursday.\nThe end result will be a chilly/gloomy day on Thursday with a \nstiff N/NE breeze and perhaps a few light showers. Storm total \nrainfall for Wednesday and Thursday has decreased from previous \nthinking...with the latest NBM indicating only a 30-50% chance \nof exceeding 0.25, but mainly along and northeast of I-74. This \nmeans the remainder of the CWA will either see no precip or much\nless than one quarter of an inch. \n\n\n**** End of Week Cool-Down ****\n\nAn upper trough is still expected to dig over the Great \nLakes/Ohio River Valley by the middle and the end of the week. \nColder air will flow southward on the back side of the departing\nlow pressure, and temperatures will temporarily drop below \nnormal for this time this year. The extended models all show \n850mb temps dropping to -5 to -8C by Friday. Therefore, after a\nbreezy/chilly day on Thursday with highs in the upper 40s and \nlower 50s, the coldest day will be Friday when highs only reach \nthe middle 40s. As high pressure builds into the region and \nwinds diminish, the coldest night of the forecast period will \narrive Friday night when lows dip into the middle to upper 20s \nacross the board, which should lead to a hard freeze. However, \nthis will be temporary. Once the upper trough shifts eastward \nand heights begin to rise, temperatures will moderate through \nthe rest of the weekend and into early next week...with readings\nclimbing back to near 60 by next Monday.",
